 ## Decisions of the owner, 2026-11-07 (D-262, D-263, D-264, D-266)
 1. **Graph rules for the new node types** stay in the Workflow management only. IF-301 keeps the v1 rules for the document form and adds only the record rules; A-90's "validate also reports ..." is read as `check_record`.
@@ -109,6 +117,7 @@ The list is closed for the fields of a type too (A-93, REQ-GTB-022): the schema
 14. **A-98 (Verify finding F-02, owner's decisions of 2026-11-08):** the nodes and edges of a context graph are kept in their own tables `context_nodes` and `context_edges`, because the Storage layer cannot know the categories of a context graph (IF-102 `put` has no such argument). Symmetric tables, so ends and members of a context graph are looked up inside the context tables. The Storage layer checks the shape only (it calls `check_record` without `categories`); the context-graph owner or the Workflow management calls it with the categories before it writes. IF-102 does not change. v2 is not merged yet, so this is part of v2.
 15. **A-98 (Verify finding F-09, owner's decision of 2026-11-08):** input that cannot be checked is `schema_break`, not `record_identity`: a record nested deeper than 200 levels names `data`, a category with an invalid schema names `type`. The limit is fixed so that mock and real code agree.
 16. **A-102 (Verify finding F-22, owner's decision of 2026-11-08):** a record with more than 100,000 values, counting a value once for every path, is `schema_break` naming `data`. A record that shares sub-objects can have an exponential number of paths; the check made `check_record` run for minutes. 100,000 is about 190 times the largest stored document in the repository (529 values). v2 is not merged yet, so this is part of v2.
+17. **A-167 (G-V4.GRAPHSTORE.CORE, D-368, D-402; owner's review of 2026-11-21):** a step or connection belongs to exactly one graph, and its record ID says which: `<graph id>/<local id>`, split at the first slash. The Graph tables refuse any other ID of a step or connection, and any header member of another graph, as `unscoped_id`. Plain IDs of v2 are refused, so this is v3; the protected tests of v2 that wrote plain IDs are rewritten with scoped IDs (same checks, same rules).
 
